            <h3>Fiumicino Terminals</h3>
            <p>FCO operates multiple terminals with distinct functions, connected by free shuttle buses and pedestrian walkways. </p>
            <h4>Terminal 1</h4>
            <p>Handles domestic Schengen flights and some international departures. </p>
            <h5>Facilities: </h5>
            <ul>
                <li>Check-in and security for ITA Airways and partner carriers</li>
                <li>Basic retail and dining options</li>
                <li>Connected to Terminal 3 via pedestrian walkway</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Terminal 3</h4>
            <p>The primary terminal for international and intercontinental flights, including all arrivals from Atlanta. </p>
            <h5>Facilities: </h5>
            <ul>
                <li>International arrivals with baggage claim</li>
                <li>Customs and immigration processing</li>
                <li>Extensive duty-free shopping and dining</li>
                <li>Train station access for Leonardo Express and regional trains</li>
                <li>Bus and taxi departure points</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Terminal 5</h4>
            <p>Previously used for select security-sensitive flights; currently undergoing repurposing. </p>
            <h3>Immigration and Customs at Fiumicino</h3>
            <p>Passengers arriving from Atlanta proceed through Schengen entry formalities. </p>
            <h4>Requirements: </h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Valid passport with minimum three months validity beyond intended departure</li>
                <li>ETIAS authorization (once implemented) or visa if required</li>
                <li>Proof of accommodation for border inspection</li>
                <li>Return or onward travel documentation</li>
                <li>Travel insurance documentation (recommended)</li>
            </ul>
            <p>US citizens enjoy visa-free access for tourism stays up to 90 days within any 180-day Schengen period. </p>
            <h3>Transportation from Fiumicino to Central Rome</h3>
            <h4>Leonardo Express</h4>
            <p>The dedicated airport train connects Fiumicino to Roma Termini, Rome's principal railway station. </p>
            <h5>Benefits: </h5>
            <ul>
                <li>Cost approximately €14</li>
                <li>Nonstop journey time 32 minutes</li>
                <li>Departures every 15 minutes during peak hours</li>
                <li>Most convenient option for Termini area hotels</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Regional Train (FL1) </h4>
            <p>A slower, more economical option connecting Fiumicino to Roma Trastevere, Ostiense, and Tiburtina stations. </p>
            <h5>Features: </h5>
            <ul>
                <li>Cost approximately €8</li>
                <li>Journey time: 45-60 minutes depending on destination</li>
                <li>Useful for travelers staying in Trastevere or Testaccio</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Taxi</h4>
            <p>Official Rome taxis operate with fixed airport rates. </p>
            <h5>Typical fares: </h5>
            <ul>
                <li>To central Rome (within Aurelian Walls): €50 flat rate</li>
                <li>To outer districts: Metered or negotiated rate</li>
                <li>Always use official white taxis with Roma insignia; avoid solicitors in arrivals</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Private Transfer and Rideshare</h4>
            <p>Pre-booked private cars, Uber (limited service in Rome), and Welcome Pickups operate from FCO. </p>
            <h4>Bus</h4>
            <p>Several coach companies including Terravision and SIT Bus Shuttle connect Fiumicino to Termini at lower cost (€5-€7) but with longer journey times and less frequency. </p>
            <h4>Car Hire</h4>
            <p>Major rental agencies maintain desks at FCO for travelers planning road trips to Tuscany, Umbria, or the Amalfi Coast. </p>
