            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: What is the cheapest time to fly from Atlanta to Rome? </h3>
            <p>January and February offer the lowest fares, typically $600-$950 round-trip in economy. For summer visits, book 45-70 days ahead and target April or October. Tuesday and Wednesday departures consistently undercut weekend prices by a meaningful margin. </p>

            <h3>Q2: Are there direct flights from Atlanta to Rome? </h3>
            <p>Delta Air Lines operates seasonal nonstop service from Atlanta to Rome Fiumicino, typically from late spring through early autumn. Flight duration is approximately 9.5 to 10 hours. During winter months, all itineraries require at least one connection through European hubs. </p>

            <h3>Q3: Do I need a visa to visit Rome from the US? </h3>
            <p>United States citizens do not require a visa for short tourist stays in Italy. The Schengen Agreement permits visits up to 90 days within any rolling 180-day period. A valid passport with at least three months' validity beyond your planned departure from Italy is required. </p>

            <h3>Q4: How much baggage can I bring on an Atlanta to Rome flight? </h3>
            <p>Most full-service carriers include one checked bag up to 50 pounds and one carry-on bag plus a personal item on transatlantic itineraries. Premium cabin passengers and elite status members typically receive additional allowances. Basic economy fares and European low-cost connections may impose separate baggage charges. </p>

            <h3>Q5: What is the best way to get from Fiumicino Airport to central Rome? </h3>
            <p>The Leonardo Express train reaches Roma Termini in 32 minutes for approximately €14 with departures every 15 minutes. The regional FL1 train costs €8 and serves Trastevere and Ostiense. Official taxis charge a flat €50 to central Rome. SIT Bus Shuttle offers economical coach service to Termini. </p>

            <h3>Q6: Can I use US dollars in Rome, or do I need euros? </h3>
            <p>Italy uses the euro exclusively, and US dollars are not accepted. ATMs are abundant throughout Rome, and credit cards work at most establishments. Carrying some cash remains advisable for smaller trattorias, coffee bars, and vendors at open-air markets that may prefer or require cash. </p>

            <h3>Q7: Is Rome safe for American tourists? </h3>
            <p>Rome is generally very safe for visitors, though petty theft and pickpocketing occur in tourist-heavy areas like the Colosseum, Trevi Fountain, and crowded buses. Secure valuables, remain aware in crowded spaces, and avoid engaging with street scammers. Violent crime against tourists is rare. </p>

            <h3>Q8: What is the best area to stay in Rome for first-time visitors? </h3>
            <p>The historic center (Centro Storico) offers ideal proximity to major monuments with excellent walking access. Trastevere provides bohemian charm and vibrant nightlife across the Tiber. Monti delivers a trendy, local atmosphere near the Colosseum. Choose based on your sightseeing priorities and evening preferences. </p>

            <h3>Q9: How early should I arrive at Atlanta airport for my Rome flight? </h3>
            <p>Arrive three hours before scheduled departure for international flights from Atlanta. During peak summer travel or holiday weekends, consider arriving 3.5 hours early. TSA PreCheck and CLEAR members can significantly reduce security wait times at the world's busiest airport. </p>

            <h3>Q10: What happens if my flight to Rome is delayed or cancelled? </h3>
            <p>Airlines must provide meals and accommodation for qualifying overnight delays depending on cause and duration. For cancellations, EU regulations mandate rebooking or refunds when flying EU-based carriers, and most major airlines extend comparable protections voluntarily. Contact your airline immediately and maintain comprehensive travel insurance for additional coverage. </p>












